{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310602,"nonce":"7yyTVYHzVjsMBVTdKhMmIJmr6/Y=","title":"FIXED 8cd8b649 (partial) — an erased generic method's Null\u003cT\u003e return was used as the BOX for reference T; runtime-validated unboxer handles ...","message":"**THE BUG.** `List\u003cT\u003e.first(): Null\u003cT\u003e` is compiled ONCE (erased), so it boxes\nwhatever bits it stored. The CALLER knew T but unboxed ONLY for primitives\n(\"Only unbox if resolved to a concrete primitive\", direct.rs / resolved_method.rs).\nFor a reference T the caller used the BOX as the value:\n- `List\u003cString\u003e.first()` printed an ADDRESS\n- `List\u003c{p:String}\u003e.first().p` read offset 0 of the box = type_id = **3**\n- haxe.Template died in parseBlock on exactly this (5 corpus rows)\n\n**THREE PRODUCERS, THREE LAYOUTS — measured, this is why one unboxer failed:**\n| producer | shape | unwrap |\n|---|---|---|\n| `first()` -\u003e `haxe_box_int_ptr(bits)` | Int tag, `value_ptr` -\u003e heap CELL holding the bits | read the cell |\n| `pop()` -\u003e `haxe_box_typed_ptr(bits, Reference=6)` -\u003e `haxe_box_reference_ptr(v, 0)` | **type_id 0**, `value_ptr` = the pointer DIRECTLY | take value_ptr |\n| `BalancedTree.get()` | RAW bits, not boxed at all | pass through |\n`first` returns `h.item` directly; `pop` returns through a local `x` — a\ndifferent lowering, different boxer. `haxe_unbox_int_ptr` on the RAW case\nDEREFERENCED THE VALUE (crashed test_balancedtree_crossmodule_mono, fault=0xa =\nthe stored 10). **Never call an unvalidated unboxer on an erased return.**\n\n**FIXED 8cd8b649:** new runtime `haxe_unbox_erased_return(ptr) -\u003e i64`:\nInt/Bool tag -\u003e cell; Float tag -\u003e f64 bits; **tag 0** or String tag -\u003e\nvalue_ptr; anything else -\u003e pass through. Tag 0 is never a real type, so it\nidentifies the reference-box shape UNAMBIGUOUSLY. Compiler side:\n`unbox_erased_generic_return` in mir/helpers/boxing.rs, called from BOTH\nreturn points of `try_resolved_function_call` (direct.rs) — that is the path\n`recv.method()` takes: HIR `Call{callee: Variable, is_method: true}`, NOT\n`try_resolved_method_call` (Field callee). The caller's T only SHAPES the\nresult register (cast to I32/Bool, bitcast to F64/pointer).\n**ONE type parameter only**: `BalancedTree\u003cK,V\u003e.get` returns V and the call\nsite cannot tell which arg the return is.\n\n**LOCAL RECEIVER FIXED fab71c8d.** `new List\u003cString\u003e()`: the New arm matched\nonly Class|Array, so an ALIASED generic (top-level `List` -\u003e haxe.ds.List)\nfell to `None` and the local was the bare alias with no args. Now typed\nthrough `lower_type` exactly as the annotation `l:List\u003cString\u003e` is, so it\nlands on the TypeId downstream already knows. **Minting a fresh\n`create_class_type` instead BROKE field resolution** (`isEmpty` mis-dispatched;\nhaxe.io.Output \"receiver's class could not be resolved\") — a fresh TypeId is\nunknown to every map keyed by TypeId.\n\n**TWO MORE GAPS the local exposed, both fixed in fab71c8d:**\n- direct.rs's `actual_return_type` for an IMPORT callee is the CALLER's type,\n  not the signature (no import return-type map exists). Passing None (\"assume\n  erased\") was TOO BROAD — it unboxed `isEmpty(): Bool` too and mangled the\n  raw Bool into a pointer (my own fixture threw). Now gated on\n  `callee_returns_type_param(symbol)`: the DECLARED return (through Optional/\n  alias) is a TypeParameter. That is the precise \"erased return\" predicate.\n- raw bits -\u003e `IrType::String` is a `build_cast(I64, String)`, NOT a bitcast\n  (vtables.rs idiom). The bitcast silently produced a bad String register.\n\n**STILL OPEN, filed 5198295:** an anon that came back through this unbox\nprints correctly but every `==` on its String field is FALSE (`lt.first().p ==\n\"rr\"` false; String `first() == \"hello\"` TRUE; plain anon and Array-held anon\nTRUE). The recovered pointer is bitcast to `convert_type(Tok)` = Ptr(Void), and\na field read on a Ptr(Void) receiver yields a register `==` does not treat as a\nString. Strictly less wrong than before (was reading the box's tag) but a\nsilent wrong branch.\n\n**Template chain now:** parseBlock's `var l = new List()` (NO type arg, inferred\nfrom use) gets its args from `infer_type_args_from_constructor`, and execute\nruns to the ctor's own `throw \"Unexpected 'false'\"` — tokens left unconsumed\nafter parseBlock. Rows 4885/5293/9372 WRONG_ANSWER. Next link.\n\n**I COMMITTED WITH A FAILING FIXTURE (091476f0, amended to fab71c8d)** — the\nfixture check and the commit were ONE shell command, so I read the FAIL after\nthe commit existed. Same trap as the very first commit of the session.\nThe fixture bug was MINE (popped the list empty, then asserted first()), but\nchasing it found the real `==` gap above. Keep check and commit SEPARATE.\n\n**I BURNED ~10 BUILDS HERE.** Wrong-path guesses (resolved_method.rs is NOT\nthe path), a python edit whose second assert failed so NOTHING was written\n(build was identical, I read \"no change\" as \"fix inert\"), and an alias-chase\nthat regressed Int. Lessons: (1) `[path]`-tag EVERY candidate emitter in ONE\nbuild; (2) after an edit script, `grep` the file for the new text BEFORE\nbuilding; (3) a fix that touches the representation gets the FULL gate, the\nsuite caught what conformance did not (207/0/1 -\u003e 206/0/2).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_erased_generic_return_used_box_as_value` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}